itary transformation parameterised via the Cayley map. Code is publicly available.\n\nC OMPLEX-V ALUED U NITARY R EPRESENTATIONS AS\n\nC LASSIFICATION H EADS FOR I MPROVED U NCERTAINTY\n\nQ UANTIFICATION IN D EEP N EURAL N ETWORKS ∗\n\nA. A. Jafari\n\nUniversity of Tartu\n\nTartu, Estonia\n\nakbar.anbar.jafari@ut.ee\n\nC. Ozcinar\n\nUniversity of Tartu\n\nTartu, Estonia\n\nchagri.ozchinar@ut.ee\n\nG. Anbarjafari\n\n3S Holding OÜ\n\nTartu 51011, Estonia\n\nshb@3sholding.com\n\nA BSTRACT\n\nModern deep neural networks achieve high predictive accuracy but remain poorly calibrated: their\n\nconﬁdence scores do not reliably reﬂect the true probability of correctness. We propose a quantum-\n\ninspired classiﬁcation head architecture that projects backbone features into a complex-valued\n\nHilbert space and evolves them under a learned unitary transformation parameterised via the Cay-\n\nley map. Through a controlled hybrid experimental design—training a single shared backbone and\n\ncomparing lightweight interchangeable heads—we isolate the effect of complex-valued unitary rep-\n\nresentations on calibration. \n2\n\nComplex-Valued Unitary Heads for Uncertainty Quantiﬁcation A. A. Jafari et al.\n\npredictions, achieving state-of-the-art uncertainty but at N×training and inference cost. Evidential Deep Learning\n\n[37] places Dirichlet priors over class probabilities. [38] and [39] provide comprehensive surveys and taxonomies of\n\naleatoric versus epistemic uncertainty. [40] evaluated uncertainty methods under dataset shift, ﬁnding that ensembles\n\nremain the most robust approach but at substantial computational cost. [41] argued for a probabilistic perspective on\n\ndeep learning generalisation. Recent work by [42] demonstrated the importance of Bayesian deep learning for reliable\n\ndetection in sensor networks, underscoring the practical need for well-calibrated models in safety-critical systems.\n\n2.3 Out-of-Distribution Detection\n\nOOD detection aims to identify inputs that differ from the training distribution. [43] established the Maximum Softmax\n\nProbability (MSP) baseline. ODIN [44] improved upon MSP through temperature scaling and input perturbation.\nlibrium convergence versus norm-preserving unitary ﬂow) to impose geometric constraints on hidden representations,\n\nsuggesting that constrained dynamics in feature space is a fruitful design principle for neural architectures.\n\n3 Methodology\n\n3.1 Preliminaries: Complex Hilbert Space\n\nDeﬁnition 1 (Complex Hilbert Space).A complex Hilbert space H of dimension d is a complete inner product space\n\nover C with the inner product ⟨ψ|φ⟩=∑d\n\ni=1 ψ i φ i for ψ,φ∈C d.\n\n3\n\nComplex-Valued Unitary Heads for Uncertainty Quantiﬁcation A. A. Jafari et al.\n\nIn quantum mechanics, the state of a system is represented by a normalised vector |ψ⟩∈H with ∥ψ∥2=1, and the\n\nprobability of observing outcome k is given by the Born rule p(k)=|⟨e k|ψ⟩|2, where {|e k⟩} are measurement basis\n\nvectors [13,14]. Time evolution is governed by unitary operators U satisfying U†U=I, which preserve the norm:\n\n∥U ψ∥=∥ψ∥.\n\n3.2 Proposed Architecture\n\nWe propose a modular classiﬁcation head that attaches to any frozen or jointly-trained backbone producing real-valued\n\n(I−S)(I+S)−1 is orthogonal: U⊤U=I. Consequently, ∥U ψ∥=∥ψ∥for all ψ.\n\nProof. Since S=−S⊤, we have (I+S)⊤=I−S. Then:\n\nU⊤U=(I+S)−1⊤(I−S)⊤(I−S)(I+S)−1\n\n= (I−S)−1(I+S)(I−S)(I+S)−1.(5)\n\nSince S is skew-symmetric, (I+S)and (I−S)commute:\n\n(I+S)(I−S) = I−S 2= (I−S)(I+S).(6)\n\nTherefore U⊤U= (I−S)−1(I−S)(I+S)(I+S)−1=I.\n\nThe Cayley parameterisation guarantees exact unitarity at every training step without requiring expensive matrix ex-\n\nponentials or retraction operations on the Stiefel manifold [50]. This is computationally efﬁcient: the matrix inverse\n\n(I+S)−1 requires O(d 3)operations, which is negligible when d≪n backbone.\n\n3.2.3 Stage 3: Probability Readout\n\nWe consider two readout mechanisms:\n\nBorn Rule Measurement. Following quantum mechanics, deﬁne C measurement vectors {m k}C\n\nk=1∈C d(one per\n\nclass) as learnable parameters, and compute:\n\np(k|f) = |⟨m k|ψ′⟩|2\n\n∑C\n\nj=1|⟨m j|ψ′⟩|2.(7)\n\n4\n\nComplex-Valued Unitary Heads for Uncertainty Quantiﬁcation A. A. Jafari et al.\n\n\nachieve 2.4×lower ECE than a standard softmax head (0.015 vs. 0.036) on CIFAR-10, representing a state-\n\nof-the-art result among non-ensemble, single-forward-pass methods.\n\n2. Contrary to initial expectations, the quantum-mechanical Born rule measurement degrades calibration (5.6×\n\nworse ECE than the magnitude readout), due to the information bottleneck identiﬁed in Proposition 2.\n\n3. Despite worse calibration, the Born rule readout best captures the structure of human perceptual uncertainty,\n\nachieving the lowest KL-divergence to CIFAR-10H soft labels among all compared methods.\n\n16\n\nComplex-Valued Unitary Heads for Uncertainty Quantiﬁcation A. A. Jafari et al.\n\n4. The calibration advantage does not transfer to OOD detection or compositional NLP, delineating the method’s\n\nscope to classiﬁcation head calibration.\n\nThe theoretical contribution—connecting norm-preserving unitary dynamics to bounded logit magnitudes and hence\n\nto calibration—provides a principled explanation for the empirical ﬁndings and suggests that geometric constraints on\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAkbar Anbar Jafari (https://www.csauthors.net/akbar-anbar-jafari/)\nciteturn31949search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Akbar Anbar Jafari ... Complex-Valued Unitary Representations as Classification Heads for Improved Uncertainty Quantification in Deep Neural Networks. ... CoRR, February, 2026\n\n[BibT_{e}X]\n\n[DOI]\n\nAkbar Anbar Jafari\n\n,\n\nCagri Ozcinar\n\n,\n\nGholamreza Anbarjafari\n\nCoRR, July, 2026\n\nComplex-Valued Unitary Representations as Classification Heads for Improved Uncertainty Quantification in Deep Neural Networks.\n\n[BibT_{e}X]\n\n[DOI]\n\nAkbar Anbar Jafari\n\n,\n\nCagri Ozcinar\n\n,\n\nGholamreza Anbarjafari\n\nCoRR, February, 2026\n\nA Multi-physics Simulation Framework for High-power Microwave Counter-unmanned Aerial System Design and Performance Evaluation.\n\n[BibT_{e}X]\n\n[DOI]\n\nAkbar Anbar Jafari\n\n,\n\nGholamreza Anbarjafari\n\nCoRR, February, 2026\n\nA Lightweight Modular Framework for Constructing Autonomous Agents Driven by Large Language Models: Design, Implementation, and Applications in AgentForge.\n\n[BibT_{e}X]\n\n[DOI]\n\nAkbar Anbar Jafari\n\n,\n\nCagri Ozcinar\n\n,\n\nGholamreza Anbarjafari\n\nCoRR, January, 2026\n\n2025\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAI Gains Reliable Confidence With New Complex System (https://quantumzeitgeist.com/ai-gains-reliable-confidence-complex-system/)\nciteturn31949search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; February 20, 2026 by Muhammad Rohail T. ... Akbar Anbar Jafari, Cagri Ozcinar, and Gholamreza Anbarjafari, working collaboratively between the University of Tartu and 3S Holding OÜ, present a novel classification head architecture inspired by quantum mechanics to address this challenge.Their research introduces complex-valued unitary representations, projecting features into a complex Hilbert space and evolving them using a learned unitary transformation.\n\n# AI Gains Reliable Confidence with New Complex System\n\nImage: Avatar photo\n\nFebruary 20, 2026 by Muhammad Rohail T.\n\nScientists are increasingly focused on improving the reliability of deep neural network predictions, as current models often exhibit poor calibration between confidence scores and actual accuracy. Akbar Anbar Jafari, Cagri Ozcinar, and Gholamreza Anbarjafari, working collaboratively between the University of Tartu and 3S Holding OÜ, present a novel classification head architecture inspired by quantum mechanics to address this challenge. Their research introduces complex-valued unitary representations, projecting features into a complex Hilbert space and evolving them using a learned unitary transformation. This approach demonstrably improves uncertainty quantification, achieving a 2.4-fold reduction in Expected Calibration Error on the CIFAR-10 dataset compared to standard softmax heads and surpassing temperature scaling methods. Furthermore, the team’s findings on the CIFAR-10H benchmark suggest these complex representations more accurately reflect human perceptual ambiguity, offering potential benefits for safety-critical applications where reliable uncertainty estimates are paramount.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNeuro-Symbolic Differentiable Reasoning (https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/neuro-symbolic-differentiable-reasoning-2421b9d1-3fba-4944-bebe-cbb57e25ba5c)\nciteturn31949search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;   * Knowledge Graph and Multi-Hop Reasoning: End-to-end QA over KBs, multi-hop inference over millions of triples via differentiable relation-following (NeuroSymActive (Fu et al., 17 Feb 2026, Cohen et al., 2019)).\nNEUMANN (Shindo et al., 2023)  | Message-passing, soft-or/soft-and  | Visual reasoning, program induction  | Classification, generalization accuracy  | 97–100% on visual and symbolic reasoning, scalable to FOL\nNSNnet (Agarwal et al., 2021)  | Policy gradient through non-diff solver  | Visual Maze, Visual Sudoku  | Task completion, classification accuracy  | ~99% cell, ~60–65% full-puzzle accuracy at low supervision\n\nThis table is based on the empirical results provided in the respective publications; it illustrates the diversity of methods, reasoning layers, and the quantitative gains brought by direct gradient flow through symbolic constraints.\n\n## 5. Sample Applications and Scalability\n\nNeuro-symbolic differentiable reasoning is effective across a range of application domains:\n\n  * Constraint-Satisfiable Generative Tasks: Sudoku, pathfinding, and preference logic puzzles, where logical consistency is enforced during generation (DDReasoner (Zhang et al., 22 Aug 2025)).\n  * Visual and Perceptual Reasoning: Image-to-symbol mapping, compositional visual QA, and semantic image interpretation (NSNnet (Agarwal et al., 2021), Neuro-Symbolic Visual Reasoning (Amizadeh et al., 2020), DF-ALC (Wu et al., 2022)).\n  * Knowledge Graph and Multi-Hop Reasoning: End-to-end QA over KBs, multi-hop inference over millions of triples via differentiable relation-following (NeuroSymActive (Fu et al., 17 Feb 2026, Cohen et al., 2019)).\n  * Scientific and Engineering Search: Closed-loop design where symbolic search over discrete topologies is coupled to differentiable optimization of continuous parameters under physical constraints (AI4S-SDS (Chen, 4 Mar 2026)).\n\nSignificant gains have been demonstrated in logical consistency, sample efficiency, robustness to perceptual or labeling noise, and the ability to scale to large symbolic domains.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBook: Positive Definite and Definitizable Functions — Professur für Stochastische Modelle / Zuverlässigkeitstheorie, Asymptotik — TU Dresden (https://tu-dresden.de/mn/math/stochastik/sasvari/die-professur/forschung/book-positive-definite-and-definitizable-functions?set_language=de)\nciteturn31949search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Errata  (Version February 13, 2025) ...   1.2 Inner product spaces defined by complex-valued functions ...   2.2 Spectral decomposition of unitary representations of abelian groups ...   6.4 Integral representation of functions f Î P^{c}(γ,k) with γ Î Γ\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n50x KV Cache Compression in Seconds via Attention Matching — Interactive Visualization (https://podcast.do-not-panic.com/viz/2026-03-09-50x-kv-cache-compression-in-seconds-via-9402c1.html)\nciteturn31949search8 [